Testing plan for lab 4
2024

This document describes the testing of lab4 and explains
the recommended order and purpose of calling several unit
testing functions from the main driver.

the 4 options described below are running the program with
    -first fit no coalescing
    -best fit no coalescing
    -first fit coalescing
    -best fit coalescing

----------------Beginning of testing plan------------------

Call unit driver 0 with all 4 options
./lab4 -u0
./lab4 -c -u0
    - the small size of bytes requested will ensure only basic
      functionality of alloc and free
    - tests best and first capability to splice blocks

Call unit driver 5 with all 4 options
./lab4 -u5
    - requests INT_MAX bytes. This should be rejected by sbrk, which is then passed on
      to morecore as a fail, which should then be passed on to Mem_alloc, which should return NULL
    - If it doesn't, go fix it as this will likely cause instability for your computer.

call unit driver 4 with all 4 options
./lab4 -u4
./lab4 -f best -u4
./lab4 -c -u4
./lab4 -c -f best -u4
    - test serves to check that stats and print are working correctly
        - special cases where list contains just dummy block
    - these functions are imparitive to testing more advanced drivers
    - expected results when called are pronting just the dummy block
      with an average, min, and max fields equal to zero in stats

call unit driver 1 with all 4 options
./lab4 -u1
./lab4 -f best -u1
./lab4 -c -u1
./lab4 -c -f best -u1
    - test fragments 1 block exactly, by adjusting free order it heavily tests
      coalescing (should reunite the entire block).
    - test also combines an additional call for part of an additional page
        - when coalescing enabled, it is possible these 2 blocks become
          joined into one block if they are sequential
        - this behavior is not guarenteed as depends on morecore allocation
    - free list should contain only the dummy block after all fragments of block one allocated


call unit driver 2 with all 4 options
./lab4 -u2
./lab4 -f best -u2
./lab4 -c -u2
./lab4 -c -f best -u2
    - test performs same fragmentation as driver 1 except frees in different order
        - ensure free list retains poessession of all blocks once returned from user
    - test also calls those same blocks again but smaller in increments of 1 unit
        - this will produce different behavior depending on which toggles are enabled
            - non-coal, first: will further fragment the fragments created in part 1,
              end free list will contain blocks of extremly small size
            - non-coal, best: similar to non-coal, first except leftover blocks will be of different size
            - coal options will always have all fragments reunited at end

note that by calling driver 1 and 2, all methods of coalessing are called

call unit driver 3 with all 4 options
./lab4 -u3
./lab4 -f best -u3
./lab4 -c -u3
./lab4 -c -f best -u3
    - driver 3 tests size requests close to the page boundary
    - tests bytes size + header equal to just below a page, equal to a page
      slightly above a page, and way above a page.
    - expected return is 6 pages in free list with 4 morecore calls



